# htpx.pin — the dotgibson/htpx commit the pairing gates read the corpus from.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. detections/README.md has always promised that each rule names the
# exact Offense fold and htpx pair that reproduces it, and ~10 rules cite an htpx blue
# entry by URL in their references:. Nothing verified any of it — an entry renamed or
# retired upstream left a rule pointing at a 404, and the only way to find out was for a
# human to click the link. dotgibson/dotfiles-Defense#196.
#
# A check against htpx main would not be a gate, for the same reason attack-data.pin
# exists: its answer would move on its own. An upstream rename would turn this repo red on
# an unrelated Tuesday, on a change nobody here made. Pinning a COMMIT makes the verdict a
# function of this repo's tree, so an htpx release becomes a deliberate bump — "here is
# what upstream renamed, here is the reference it breaks" — reviewed in a PR.
#
# NO sha256 FIELD, and that is not an omission. The ATT&CK pin needs one because it names
# a loose file over HTTPS; this names a git commit, and git's own object hashing already
# binds the sha to the exact tree. Verifying the checkout's HEAD against the pin IS the
# digest check, and detections/htpx-corpus.sh does it on every run.
#
# TO BUMP: change sha (and version/tag, which are human provenance only — the tooling
# compares sha and nothing else), then `make htpx-report` to regenerate HTPX-COVERAGE.md and
# `make htpx` to gate it. The claim gate fails until the references this repo writes agree
# with the corpus at the new sha, and the report diff is the point of bumping deliberately:
# it is the only place a change in the boundary's shape is visible.
#
# YOU WILL BE TOLD WHEN A BUMP IS OWED. .github/workflows/htpx-drift.yml asks weekly whether
# upstream's entries/ has moved, and files one issue when it has. It stays quiet for commits
# that do not touch entries/ — only two gates read this corpus and both read entries/ alone,
# so a pin behind by a README edit is behind by nothing. It leads with any entry this repo
# NAMES that upstream no longer has, because the gate cannot see that: the gate reads the
# pinned sha, while the references: URLs in the rules point at /blob/main/, so such an entry
# is a live 404 for a reader while CI is still green.
#
# Fields mirror dotfiles-Offense's companion.lock, which vendors the same upstream as a
# git subtree. This repo deliberately does NOT vendor it: nothing here builds from the
# corpus, two gates read it, and a ~200-file subtree plus a second sync obligation is a
# steep price for that. #196 chose the pin.
#
# ── THIS SHA NEED NOT MATCH dotfiles-Offense'S ────────────────────────────────
# Offense is the other consumer of the same corpus, and it tracks htpx differently: it
# VENDORS the tree at offensive/companion/ and records the revision in companion.lock. So
# the fleet holds two independently-pinned htpx revisions, advanced by two different
# triggers — Offense's by htpx's fan-out on a RELEASE TAG, this one by a reviewed manual
# bump.
#
# They drift apart routinely, and that is FINE. A release fans out to Offense
# automatically and leaves this pin alone until someone bumps it on purpose — which is the
# whole reason for pinning: the gates' verdict must not move on its own. The lag
# self-corrects at the next bump.
#
# It is written down because the mismatch LOOKS like a bug when you first meet it —
# Offense's PURPLE-TEAM.md rendering one snapshot of the corpus while HTPX-COVERAGE.md
# here describes another, with both repos' gates green. Neither is wrong; they are
# describing different revisions. Do NOT "fix" it by making this pin track Offense's sha
# or htpx's releases: that trades a reviewed decision for a cadence set elsewhere, which
# is exactly what the pin exists to prevent. dotgibson/dotfiles-Offense#251.
repo	dotgibson/htpx
branch	main
sha	7ea71779365c5ca89824e1e4852462eafc111f85
version	3.1.0
tag	v3.1.0
